(a) Uncontrolled stormwater drainage/discharge may have a significant, adverse impact on the health, safety and welfare of the county and the quality of life of its citizens. More specifically, surface water runoff can carry pollutants and nutrients into receiving waters. The potential impacts of these pollutant and nutrient loadings include:
(1) Sediment which may alter natural systems, scouring vital habitat and smothering stream life;
(2) Increased bacteria which may pose a significant health risk;
(3) Excessive nutrients which may accelerate algal growth and eutrophication of receiving waters, adversely affecting flora and fauna;
(4) Pollutants and nutrients which may negatively affect vital habitat for fish, birds, wildlife and native plant communities;
(5) Metals which may be toxic to aquatic life;
(6) Oil and grease which may reduce oxygen levels; and
(7) Temperature of receiving waters may increase, which may adversely affect animal and plant habitat.
(b) Uncontrolled stormwater drainage may increase the incidence of flooding and the level of floods which occur, endangering roads, other public and private property and human life.
(c) Altered land surfaces may change the rate and volume of runoff which may result in the following:
(1) Streambanks may erode and slump, resulting in widening of streams;
(2) Tree root systems may be undercut;
(3) Erosion rates may increase; and
(4) Streambeds may become more uniform and shallow, providing less varied aquatic habitats.
(d) Adverse water quality and quantity consequences as set forth in this section could result in substantial economic losses. Potential losses include, but are not limited to, increased water treatment cost, as well as state and federal fines associated with water quality violations.
(1) Many future problems can be avoided through proper stormwater management.
(2) Every parcel of real property, both public and private, either uses or benefits from the maintenance of the county's storm sewer system.
(3) Current and anticipated growth will contribute to and increase the need for improvement and maintenance of the storm sewer system.
(Ord. No. 00-5, § 2-6.1-151, 6-20-2000)

